<figure class="viz" id="viz-roads">
  <p class="chart-title">Low road, high road</p>
  <div class="roads">
    <div class="road road-fast">
      <p class="road-kicker">Fast, crude</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <span class="node-name">Sound</span>
          <span class="node-note">The bang, the shriek, the lurch</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="node-name">Thalamus</span>
          <span class="node-note">A quick relay</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="node-name">Amygdala</span>
          <span class="node-note">Defensive response, before a story</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="road road-slow">
      <p class="road-kicker">Slow, detailed</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <span class="node-name">Sound</span>
          <span class="node-note">The same input</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="node-name">Thalamus</span>
          <span class="node-note">Then onward, not straight across</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="node-name">Cortex</span>
          <span class="node-note">Enough detail to name it</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="node-name">Amygdala</span>
          <span class="node-note">A slower update of the same alarm</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="roads-source">
      The amygdala drives the defensive response. Cortical networks produce the conscious feeling of fear.
    </p>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    Simplified schematic of the two paths.{' '}
    <a href="https://www.cell.com/current-biology/fulltext/S0960-9822(20)30492-9">LeDoux, 2020</a>
    {' '}and{' '}
    <a href="https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ajp.2016.16030353">LeDoux and Pine, 2016</a>.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
